Source work
-----------

    Thomas D. Gillespie, "Fundamentals of Vehicle Dynamics"
    (SAE, 1992; Chinese translation by 赵六奇 / 金达锋,
     Tsinghua University Press / 清华大学出版社, 2006)

    Chapters distilled: all ten — 1 axle loads, 2 acceleration, 3 braking,
    4 road loads, 5 ride, 6 steady-state steering, 7 suspension,
    8 steering systems, 9 rollover, 10 tyres.

This package is an independent engineering distillation prepared for
commentary, technical explanation and criticism. It is not affiliated with,
authorised by, sponsored by, or endorsed by the author, the translators, or
the publisher. The book is named here for attribution and criticism only.


What is and is not reproduced
-----------------------------

WHAT THE PACKAGE DOES NOT CONTAIN:

  * no continuous passage of the book's prose of ten characters or more;
  * no chapter, section, page, table or figure of the book;
  * no reproduction of the book's typographical arrangement.

Every "原书要点" (source-point) line in the capability cards is a SUMMARY
written for this package, keyed to a page or section reference so a reader can
check it against a copy of the book. Those summaries were produced by
paraphrase, then checked mechanically: each was compared against a normalised
form of the whole book (178,541 characters) and rewritten until its longest
run of consecutive characters shared with the source was under ten.

WHAT THE PACKAGE DOES CONTAIN, AND WHY THAT IS FINE:

  * physical relationships, formulae and units — not protectable subject
    matter;
  * numerical data, typical values and test results — facts are not
    protectable subject matter;
  * the structure of the capability cards, the routing, the recomputations,
    and the defect analyses — original work of this package.

The value of the package lies in the last group. Roughly 77 distinct defects
in the source are documented — misprints, OCR damage, internally contradictory
passages, and two figures whose numbers cannot be reconciled. Those findings
are the package's own contribution and exist precisely to warn a reader away
from the printed text's errors, not to substitute for it.


How the source was obtained and handled
---------------------------------------

The book reached this pipeline only as a page scan. Pages were OCR'd, and
display formulas were cropped and transcribed to LaTeX by a vision model. The
process introduced errors; where detected they are recorded in the cards
rather than silently repaired. A representative sample of what is recorded:

  Chapter 1   eq (1-5) inertia term printed with the wrong sign; the convention
              for b and c runs opposite to several other texts.
  Chapter 2   Example 2.2 states 88 ft/s in first gear, which is physically
              impossible for that engine; 38.7 ft/s is consistent.
  Chapter 3   a 500 psi row in the efficiency table gives 0.6617 where the
              table's own data give 0.617; the note about efficiency falling
              with deceleration contradicts the table.
  Chapter 4   the fuel-economy chain of Example 4.5-1 does not close
              (215 hp x 0.35 = 75.3 lb/h against 82.6 lb/h printed).
  Chapter 5   eq (5-11) is missing entirely; eqs (5-15) and (5-16) are
              unusable after OCR damage; the absolute scale of G0 contradicts
              the axis range of Fig 5.2 by two orders of magnitude.
  Chapter 6   Example 1 prints a rear axle load of 1932 lb where 1552 lb is
              required; p179 and p210 disagree on roll-centre direction.
  Chapter 7   the approximation e/d ~ 2h/L overstates the exact value by 25.5%
              on the book's own example; eqs (7-12) and (7-26) carry opposite
              load-transfer signs, making the four-wheel-drive branch unusable.
  Chapter 8   eq (8-4) writes sin(delta) where the text and figure note give
              cos(delta); eq (8-17) is unrecoverable and is not reconstructed.
  Chapter 9   p241 says the roll rate should be as HIGH as possible, which
              contradicts eq (9-6); FMVSS 1.2 is cited by the book without any
              standard number, and none has been invented here.
  Chapter 10  the cornering-coefficient quantity is dimensioned 1/deg, not
              dimensionless as sometimes restated; several figures carry no
              data table and are therefore read qualitatively only.

Per chapter the package also records what it deliberately did NOT do: where a
figure has no data table the card states that no numerical readout is
available, rather than estimating one; where a formula is unrecoverable the
card retains only the boundaries that can be established and says so.


